### Which @angular/* package(s) are relevant/related to the feature request?

compiler, core

### Description

A clip editor has one selected clip, or none. While editing clip A, a user types a draft caption into a native input. If A's title changes, the input should keep its draft. If the user selects B, the new editor should start empty. Both selections satisfy the same `@if`, so Angular updates the bindings on the existing element and B inherits A's draft.

The same distinction comes up in a media preview. A one-shot GIF can keep its image element when its loading indicator goes away. Selecting a new playback session can give the entire preview fresh elements and fresh component-local state, even if the two sessions use the same asset URL. If two images need to coexist while one preloads, that's a collection and a tracked `@for` still fits.

### Proposed solution

Allow a conditional branch to declare what identifies its current view. One possible spelling:

```html
@if (selected(); as clip; track clip.id) {
  <input [attr.aria-label]="clip.title">
}
```

- While the branch is active and the key stays equal, update its bindings and keep its DOM nodes and child component instances. This includes replacing the model object with a new object carrying the same ID.
- When that branch's key changes, destroy its old view (including child teardown) and create a fresh one in the same render. Returning from B to A creates a new A view. A changed key intentionally discards local state such as focus or an unsaved draft, so the key should be stable for the life of the selection.
- Evaluate the key only for the selected branch, with the condition alias in scope. Leaving the branch destroys its view as usual, including when the condition becomes falsy. An unkeyed branch keeps today's behavior.

A design choice is whether the key should always compare the last and current evaluated values, including after an in-place object mutation. That would make a key a deliberate reset signal and would differ from `@for`'s [documented same-reference behavior](https://angular.dev/guide/templates/control-flow#why-is-track-in-for-blocks-important).

### Alternatives considered

With immutable clip records, this works today:

```html
@let clip = selected();
@for (item of clip ? [clip] : []; track item.id) {
  <input [attr.aria-label]="item.title">
}
```

The loop and temporary collection communicate “list” where the data model says “one optional selection.” Resetting the input's value explicitly also works for this example. It gets harder when the branch contains several stateful elements or child components. A structural directive could manage the same embedded-view lifetime, at the cost of adding an app-level directive for a template operation.

Related: #51302 proposed reloading a child component. This request puts the key on the parent's conditional branch, which can contain native elements and several children.

### Reproduction on Angular 22.3.0

A standalone Angular TestBed example compares actual DOM node identity and native input values. All four tests passed in a fresh Angular CLI project (CLI 22.2.8, Vitest 5.1.1, jsdom). The compiler also reports `Unrecognized conditional parameter "track clip.id"` for the proposed spelling.

<details>
<summary>Runnable spec</summary>

Save as `src/app/view-identity.spec.ts` in a standard Angular 22 CLI app with Vitest, then run `npx ng test --watch=false --include src/app/view-identity.spec.ts`.

```ts
import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { TestBed } from '@angular/core/testing';
import { parseTemplate } from '@angular/compiler';
import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';

interface Clip {
  id: string;
  title: string;
}

@Component({
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    @let clip = selected();
    @if (clip) {
      <input class="plain" [attr.aria-label]="clip.title">
    }
    @for (item of clip ? [clip] : []; track item.id) {
      <input class="keyed" [attr.aria-label]="item.title">
    }
  `,
})
class ClipEditor {
  readonly selected = signal<Clip | null>({ id: 'A', title: 'First clip' });
}

function mountEditor() {
  const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(ClipEditor);
  const host: HTMLElement = fixture.nativeElement;
  fixture.detectChanges();

  function input(kind: 'plain' | 'keyed') {
    const node = host.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('input.' + kind);
    if (!node) throw new Error('Expected the ' + kind + ' input to be mounted');
    return node;
  }

  function select(clip: Clip | null) {
    fixture.componentInstance.selected.set(clip);
    fixture.detectChanges();
  }

  return { host, input, select };
}

describe('one selected clip', () => {
  it('updates bindings but keeps both inputs and their drafts for the same ID', () => {
    const { input, select } = mountEditor();
    const plain = input('plain');
    const keyed = input('keyed');
    plain.value = 'Draft A';
    keyed.value = 'Draft A';

    select({ id: 'A', title: 'Updated title' });

    expect(input('plain')).toBe(plain);
    expect(input('keyed')).toBe(keyed);
    for (const node of [plain, keyed]) {
      expect(node.getAttribute('aria-label')).toBe('Updated title');
      expect(node.value).toBe('Draft A');
    }
  });

  it('keeps the plain input but replaces the tracked input when the ID changes', () => {
    const { input, select } = mountEditor();
    const plain = input('plain');
    const keyed = input('keyed');
    plain.value = 'Draft A';
    keyed.value = 'Draft A';

    select({ id: 'B', title: 'Second clip' });

    expect(input('plain')).toBe(plain);
    expect(plain.getAttribute('aria-label')).toBe('Second clip');
    expect(plain.value).toBe('Draft A');
    expect(input('keyed')).not.toBe(keyed);
    expect(input('keyed').getAttribute('aria-label')).toBe('Second clip');
    expect(input('keyed').value).toBe('');
  });

  it('removes both inputs on clear and creates fresh ones when the same clip returns', () => {
    const { host, input, select } = mountEditor();
    const plain = input('plain');
    const keyed = input('keyed');

    select(null);
    expect(host.querySelectorAll('input')).toHaveLength(0);

    select({ id: 'A', title: 'First clip' });
    expect(input('plain')).not.toBe(plain);
    expect(input('keyed')).not.toBe(keyed);
  });

  it('reports the proposed track clause as an unsupported conditional parameter', () => {
    const template = '@if (selected(); as clip; track clip.id) { <input> }';
    const { errors } = parseTemplate(template, 'example.html');
    expect(errors?.map(error => error.msg)).toContain('Unrecognized conditional parameter "track clip.id"');
  });
});
```

</details>

### Which @angular/* package(s) are relevant/related to the feature request?

compiler-cli

### Description

Templates sometimes declare a `@let` only for its side effect: reading a signal so an `OnPush` view re-renders when it changes. For example, a form control whose value is read through a `typedMapper` pipe needs the view to re-render on `valueChanges`:

    @let _valueTick = getValueChangesSignal(valueControl)();

`_valueTick` is never read, so NG8112 reports it on every build. The warning is correct about the read, but the declaration is intentional. An unused `@let` expression is still evaluated on every template update, which is exactly why it works as a dependency.

Today there are only two options:

- Suppress `unusedLetDeclaration` for the whole project, which also hides genuinely dead `@let`s.
- Move the read into a component method that calls the signal and returns the value. That works, but it puts template-only plumbing into the class, and the warning gives no hint that the original was intentional.

TypeScript (`noUnusedLocals`/`noUnusedParameters`) and ESLint (`no-unused-vars` with `varsIgnorePattern`) both let a naming convention mark a binding as intentionally unused. NG8112 has no equivalent.

### Proposed solution

Let a check's entry in `extendedDiagnostics.checks` take an object as well as a category string. For `unusedLetDeclaration`, that object would add an `ignorePrefix` option:

```json
"angularCompilerOptions": {
  "extendedDiagnostics": {
    "checks": {
      "unusedLetDeclaration": {
        "category": "warning",
        "ignorePrefix": "_"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

- `ignorePrefix` defaults to unset, so the diagnostic behaves exactly as today unless a project opts in.
- The existing string form (`"unusedLetDeclaration": "warning"`) stays valid.
- In `UnusedLetDeclarationCheck.run()`, a declaration is skipped when its name starts with the configured prefix:

```ts
if (!usedLetDeclarations.has(decl) && !(ignorePrefix && decl.name.startsWith(ignorePrefix))) {
```

We've been running the hard-coded `_` version of this as a local patch on 22.2.4. It suppresses only the intentional declarations, and every other unused `@let` is still reported.

### Alternatives considered

- **Hard-code `_` as the ignore prefix** (no config). Simpler, and it matches TypeScript, but it changes behaviour for projects that already use `_`-prefixed `@let`s and expect the warning.
- **A regex option, like ESLint's `varsIgnorePattern`.** More flexible, but a prefix covers the real use case and is easier to validate in `angularCompilerOptions`.
- **Suppress the check per project.** Loses the diagnostic for real dead code.
- **A template-level opt-out comment.** Noisier than a naming convention, and there's no precedent for one in Angular templates.

### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

zone.js

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

Inside `fakeAsync()`, cancelling a timer task that was scheduled **before** the test entered `fakeAsync()` is routed (via the `ProxyZoneSpec`) to `FakeAsyncTestZoneSpec.onCancelTask`. That method assumes it owns the task and does

```js
case 'setTimeout':
case 'requestAnimationFrame':
  return this._clearTimeout(task.data['handleId']);
case 'setInterval':
  return this._clearInterval(task.data['handleId']);
```

i.e. it removes whichever **fake** timer has the same numeric id as the **real** timer being cancelled. It never checks that it scheduled the task.

Fake ids are taken from the native `setTimeout` id pool (#54600, the fix for #54323), so they don't collide with real `setTimeout` ids. `requestAnimationFrame` ids come from a separate native counter, so they can. `Scheduler.nextId` is seeded once, at load time, from a native `setTimeout` id. In a new CLI project's Karma page that is `1`, and the page's first rAF is also `1`.

Angular hits this path by itself when TestBed is zoneless. `TestBed.createComponent()` in a `beforeEach` (outside `fakeAsync`) makes the change-detection scheduler arm its `setTimeout` + `requestAnimationFrame` race (`scheduleCallbackWithRafRace`) in the ProxyZone. The end of the test's first `fixture.detectChanges()` inside `fakeAsync()` then cancels that pending callback, after `ngOnInit` has run. If a fake timer was queued during that `detectChanges()` (e.g. an RxJS `debounceTime` set up in `ngOnInit`, whose `AsyncAction` uses `setInterval`) and got the colliding id, `cancelAnimationFrame(1)` silently deletes it from the fake queue, and `tick()` never runs it. Logged in the reproduction below: real `setTimeout` 4 and rAF 1 armed by `createComponent()`, `Scheduler.nextId` 1 on entering `fakeAsync()`, `cancelAnimationFrame(1)` during `detectChanges()`, then 0 queued fake timers while `pendingPeriodicTimers` still lists the interval.

TestBed is zoneless by default, but the CLI's generated test entry point (`@angular/build`, for Karma and for Vitest) adds `provideZoneChangeDetection()` whenever zone.js is loaded. With zone change detection, `createComponent()` arms no rAF in this case and nothing collides. So the Angular case needs a zoneless TestBed: a zoneless app whose tests still use `fakeAsync()` (`provideZonelessChangeDetection()` in the test), or a test entry point that calls `initTestEnvironment()` without zone providers.

The visible symptom is an order-dependent test. It fails every time when it is the first test the browser runs (e.g. focused, or run with `--include`), and passes once an earlier test has armed a rAF or a fake timer. With Jasmine's random order a full run fails intermittently: alongside one other spec it failed in 1 of 4 runs.

The same code path also means the real timer is never cleared natively: `onCancelTask` doesn't call `delegate.cancelTask()` for these sources, so a real `setTimeout` cancelled inside `fakeAsync()` still fires later. In Angular's case that's harmless because the scheduler also swaps its callback for a no-op.

### Reproduction

Repository: https://github.com/jayedge/zonejs-fakeasync-cancel-repro

A new CLI project (`ng new --test-runner=karma --zoneless=false`) plus two specs. Run each **on its own**, so it is the first test the browser runs:

```
npx ng test --watch=false --include src/app/debounce-cancel.spec.ts
npx ng test --watch=false --include src/app/raf-cancel.spec.ts
```

`src/app/debounce-cancel.spec.ts`:

```ts
import { Component, OnInit, provideZonelessChangeDetection } from '@angular/core';
import { ComponentFixture, TestBed, fakeAsync, tick } from '@angular/core/testing';
import { BehaviorSubject } from 'rxjs';
import { debounceTime } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Component({ selector: 'app-search', template: '{{ result }}' })
class SearchComponent implements OnInit {
  readonly query = new BehaviorSubject<string>('');
  result = '';

  ngOnInit(): void {
    // BehaviorSubject replays '' on subscribe, so the debounce arms its setInterval during ngOnInit.
    this.query.pipe(debounceTime(150)).subscribe((value) => (this.result = value));
  }
}

describe('fakeAsync with zoneless Angular change detection', () => {
  let fixture: ComponentFixture<SearchComponent>;

  beforeEach(() => {
    TestBed.configureTestingModule({ imports: [SearchComponent], providers: [provideZonelessChangeDetection()] });
    fixture = TestBed.createComponent(SearchComponent); // outside fakeAsync()
  });

  it('fires a debounce armed during the first detectChanges()', fakeAsync(() => {
    fixture.detectChanges();
    fixture.componentInstance.query.next('x');
    tick(150);
    expect(fixture.componentInstance.result).toBe('x'); // fails when this is the first test: ''
  }));
});
```

`src/app/raf-cancel.spec.ts` (zone.js only, no TestBed):

```ts
import { fakeAsync, tick } from '@angular/core/testing';

declare const Zone: any; // zone.js global; a new CLI project has no typings for it

describe('fakeAsync, zone.js only', () => {
  let rafId: number;

  beforeEach(() => {
    rafId = requestAnimationFrame(() => {}); // outside fakeAsync()
  });

  it('keeps a fake timer whose id equals the cancelled rAF id', fakeAsync(() => {
    // Force the id coincidence (private static). In the Angular case the first fake id and the first rAF id are both 1.
    (Zone.current.get('FakeAsyncTestZoneSpec') as any)._scheduler.constructor.nextId = rafId;
    let runs = 0;
    const intervalId = window.setInterval(() => runs++, 150);
    expect(intervalId).toBe(rafId); // passes: the collision is in place

    cancelAnimationFrame(rafId); // cancels the REAL rAF...
    tick(150);

    expect(runs).toBe(1); // ...but the fake interval is gone: 0
    clearInterval(intervalId);
  }));
});
```

Run on its own, the first failed 3 of 3 times. The second forces the collision, so it fails in every run, alone or not. With the workaround below loaded, both pass.

First seen in an application's suite on Angular 21.2 with zone.js 0.16.2, whose own test entry point leaves TestBed zoneless.


